## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Sample survey methods)
## DBsection(Concepts)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('sampling')
## TitleText1(Introduction to Statistics: Think & Do)
## EditionText1(4.1)
## AuthorText1(Stevens)
## Section1(1.2)
## Problem1(9)
## TitleText2(Mind on Statistics)
## EditionText2(5)
## AuthorText2(Utts and Heckard)
## Section2(5.4 5.5)
## Problem2(50 52 59 60)
## Author(Doug Torrance)
## Institution(Piedmont)

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',     'PGML.pl',
    'PGchoicemacros.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

@options = (
    q/The commissioner randomly selects 2 teams from all 30 MLB teams
    and has everyone on that team drug tested./,
    q/At a border crossing, every 15th car is searched./,
    q/I randomly select 10 male and 10 female students to be in a survey
    on whether or not they like my statistics class./,
    q/In a survey about a given movie, a surveyor stands at the exit and
    interviews people as they come out of the theater. When one survey is
    completed the surveyor picks the next person coming out of the theater./,
    q/A quality assurance person randomly selects one box of CD's coming off the
    production line and tests all of the CD's in that box for defects./,
    q/In a survey of TV viewing preferences 100 people are chosen from the age
    brackets of 15-20, 21-25, 26-30, .../,
    q/In a factory producing television sets, every 100th set produced is
    inspected./,
    q/A class of 200 students is seated in 10 rows of 20 students per row.
    Three students are randomly selected from every row./,
    q/An airline company randomly chooses one flight from a list of all
    international flights taking place that day.  All passengers on that selected
    flight are asked to fill out a survey on meal satisfaction./,
    q/To assess passenger satisfaction, an airline distributed questionnaires to
    100 passengers in the airline's frequent flyer lounge./,
    q/A political scientist surveys the 80 people in a class he teaches to
    evaluate student political views./
);

@answers = (
    'cluster', 'systematic',  'stratified', 'convenience',
    'cluster', 'stratified',  'systematic', 'stratified',
    'cluster', 'convenience', 'convenience'
);

@choices        = random_subset(2, 0 .. $#answers);
@chosen_options = @options[@choices];
@chosen_answers = @answers[@choices];

$a = RadioButtons([ 'systematic', 'convenience', 'stratified', 'cluster' ],
    @chosen_answers[0]);
$b = RadioButtons([ 'systematic', 'convenience', 'stratified', 'cluster' ],
    @chosen_answers[1]);

BEGIN_PGML
Identify the type of sampling used (systematic, convenience, stratified, or
cluster).

a) [$chosen_options[0]]

    [_]{$a}

b) [$chosen_options[1]]

    [_]{$b}


END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that the different types of sampling are

- *Systematic Sampling* Selecting every k-th individual from an ordered list after a random starting point.
- *Convenience Sampling* Selecting individuals who are easiest to reach or most readily available.
- *Stratified Sampling* Dividing the population into subgroup strata (e.g., by age, gender) and taking a random sample from every single group.
- *Cluster Sampling* Dividing the population into naturally occurring clusters (e.g., schools, cities), randomly picking a few entire clusters, and sampling everyone within them.

1) [$a]
2) [$b]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT('This selects 2 of 11 different examples');

ENDDOCUMENT();
